---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="Where the flags and facts on Flagdex come from, and how entries are written.">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1 style="margin-top: 2rem">How this site works</h1>

    <h2>Three levels of detail</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="tier-dot tier-1"></span><strong>Full entries</strong> are written by hand: every colour and symbol, the
      flag's origin and evolution, notable facts, look-alikes and sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="tier-dot tier-2"></span><strong>Rich entries</strong> have a shorter written description with the main
      colours, symbols and dates.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="tier-dot tier-3"></span><strong>Catalogue entries</strong> are imported from Wikidata: the image, who
      used it, dates, and where recorded, the colours and objects it shows. They have no written description yet.
    </p>

    <h2>Official, traditional and popular meanings</h2>
    <p>Flag symbolism is full of explanations with very different standing, so every stated meaning is labelled:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="basis basis-official">Official</span> set out in law, a constitution or by the adopting body.</li>
      <li>
        <span class="basis basis-traditional">Traditional</span> long-standing and well documented, but not formally
        defined.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="basis basis-popular">Popular reading</span> a common explanation with no official basis (for example,
        "red stands for blood shed" where no law says so).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sources and licences</h2>
    <p>
      Catalogue data comes from <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/">Wikidata</a>, which is public domain (CC0). Flag images
      are loaded from <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/">Wikimedia Commons</a>; most national flags are in the public
      domain, and each flag page links to its file page with licence details. Written entries are original text and cite
      their sources, including national laws and constitutions, <a href="https://www.crwflags.com/fotw/flags/">Flags of the
      World</a> and Wikipedia.
    </p>
    <p>
      Colour hex codes are screen approximations. Many official specifications use Pantone or CMYK values, and different
      sources give slightly different conversions.
    </p>

    <h2>Your progress</h2>
    <p>
      Quiz progress is saved in this browser only. Flags you miss are asked more often, and flags you haven't seen recently
      come back round.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
